Ray Dillinger <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
>
> At many times in (real) human history, battles have been 
> decided by whose smiths were able to work the harder 
> metal, so legends about heroes able to extract the sword 
> (steel or earlier harder-than-then-extant metal) from 
> the stone (ore) are with us since the bronze age.  It 
> is not a coincidence, for the most well-known example, 
> that Arthur came to power in England at about the same 
> time steelworking was introduced.  So yes, a harder 
> metal that only a small set of people knows how to work 
> is a standard storytelling element with roots as old as 
> war. Tolkien happened to call it "Mithril."  Although 
> others have been using it, that particular word for it 
> is his.  I will probably have "Adamantine" in the game - 
> a name in use for very hard steel as early as the 17th 
> century, more recently picked up by Marvel Comics as 
> "Adamantium" making it familiar to modern gamers. 

Interesting. I didn't know that.

> <mumak, oliphaunt> 
> Gigantic relative or ancestor of elephants, known as 
> Mumak/Mumakil  by the Rohirrim and as Oliphaunts by 
> the Hobbits.  Anyway, both words are Tolkien's.  
> You have to remember Tolkien was telling a story about 
> the Rohirrim and the Hobbits, and he gave us *their* 
> words for an animal we'd probably call a Mastodon or 
> Mammoth. Using their words is using Tolkien's work.  

Mumak is probably Tolkien's own invention.

> I can use Mastodons and Mammoths - that's not the 
> problem.  But I shouldn't use Tolkien's words for them. 

Oliphaunt is not Tolkien's word. It is an older spelling of elephant.
http://www.etymonline.com/index.php?term=elephant

Considering how incoherent earlier spelling was you'd probably find
any possible combination of o,i,e and ph,f replaced with each other.

This is of course a general problem with how Tolien created his word.
Sometimes he used old spellings, sometimes words from other languages,
sometimes he used generic words for something specific and sometimes
he used a artificial linguistic change on words.

To even determine completely what can be copyrighted and what not of
Tolkien's extensive work would be a nightmare.

